#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "4968972d-3bc9-5100-aa70-46213e0c3581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5028" :question/problem "Is $x=3$ a root of the polynomial $2x^3+8x^2-2x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2(3)^3+8(3)^2-2(3)-8 &= \\\n\t\t2(27)+8(9)-6-8 &= \\\n\t\t54+72-14 &= \\\n\t\t112\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$\n\nTherefore, $3$ is *not* a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "c1830274-3260-5bf5-aef1-c855a89efbf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5030" :question/problem "Find $p$ if $3$ is a root of $x^3-3x+p.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=3$ is a root, we have $f(3)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=3,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{3}-3(3)+p & =0 \\\\ 27-9+p & =0 \\\\ p & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26aca2b6-27fd-5137-9f5a-b761d0340f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513639} {:answer/id #uuid "bfaf3574-1d71-5d97-bc40-fa0a286b8c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514038} {:answer/id #uuid "c90944c5-320d-596d-8f3d-d4daa4e170c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514058} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d73040-b6a3-590a-8f1a-f21f92d2e834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514132} {:answer/id #uuid "fed866e1-3125-5187-99bc-41e67b687920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d73040-b6a3-590a-8f1a-f21f92d2e834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514132} :answer-field/id #uuid "973ef37b-f85d-515c-b5ff-cf8e3417a87e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d0ba8c-f3fb-52e6-bc68-8e1659694309" :question/math-academy-id "q-114096" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-21x+45?$\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=4$\n\nIII. $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}-{3}^{2}-21(3)+45 & = \\\\ 27-9-63+45 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $3$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3}-{4}^{2}-21(4)+45 & = \\\\ 64-16-84+45 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3}-{5}^{2}-21(5)+45 & = \\\\ 125-25-105+45 & = \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $5$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=5,$ so the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ca2c7d-d240-55ed-8330-bebe574fc9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513711} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5f3c4e-7117-5231-97ac-d46e036354a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513733} {:answer/id #uuid "75caf8e3-d457-5667-b62f-35a6de77d769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513844} {:answer/id #uuid "cf097d6d-7e5d-5696-967e-7db28b75519d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d013c3-4d16-528c-96a4-cec120511278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a5f3c4e-7117-5231-97ac-d46e036354a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513733} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee58cba8-ea3f-5e96-a12e-8748931de619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6dc4cf16-d154-5217-ba51-01445eca1e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14001" :question/problem "Find $p$ if $5$ is a root of $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+4px-10.$" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=5$ is a root, we have $f(5)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=5,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(5)^{2}+4p(5)-10 & =0 \\\\ 6(25)+20p-10 & =0 \\\\ 150+20p-10 & =0 \\\\ 140+20p & =0 \\\\ 20p & =-140 \\\\ p & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c26992c-eeda-521a-91c1-51ff8ccc188a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513605} {:answer/id #uuid "6113f01d-038a-5ec5-b1bc-33c2dc4ce6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513795} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8f7c9e-2f92-5ee6-b16c-88a200fa7761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513903} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c1d10e-3180-570f-b6a8-67238002e359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513962} {:answer/id #uuid "fae75ffa-784f-514f-a697-d19f80fd75b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae75ffa-784f-514f-a697-d19f80fd75b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514185} :answer-field/id #uuid "2382d852-7536-52f3-ba9c-8de134bcd26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18a12e6a-b89b-57fc-8194-94116cfc4da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37763" :question/problem "Find $a$ if $-2$ is a root of ${x}^{4}+a{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+60x+100.$" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=-2$ is a root, we have $f(-2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=-2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{4}+a(-2)^{3}-11(-2)^{2}+60(-2)+100 & =0 \\\\ 16-8a-44-120+100 & =0 \\\\ -8a-48 & =0 \\\\ -8a & =48 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b8fb14-c0e0-59ff-9dcc-0cfee40893f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067067} {:answer/id #uuid "0501fd5c-c073-5ad6-b7e1-e58ff883086a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067068} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9c9a20-208b-5bf1-89b3-8f27461cb543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067069} {:answer/id #uuid "58197747-933e-54cf-ae88-668f447c54a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067070} {:answer/id #uuid "7274c8da-3be2-5d2d-aed6-509173699a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58197747-933e-54cf-ae88-668f447c54a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067070} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fd6c60-d87e-5fe2-a103-1257520e8a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b7c4071-ca3e-544d-9aa0-e1f8f9fa4fb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3895" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{4}-13{x}^{2}+36?$\n\nI. $x=4$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=-4$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{4}-13(4)^{2}+36 & = \\\\ 256-13(16)+36 & = \\\\ 256-208+36 & = \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-13(2)^{2}+36 & = \\\\ 16-13(4)+36 & = \\\\ 16-52+36 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $-4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-13(-4)^{2}+36 & = \\\\ 256-13(16)+36 & = \\\\ 256-208+36 & = \\\\ 84\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=-4,$ so the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}]}
